.TH HOLY-AGENT 7 "2026-10-03" "holy" "Holy"
.SH NAME
holy-agent \- the build, test and reporting contract for this repository
.SH DESCRIPTION
Holy is built, tested and reported with ordinary tools. This page names the targets a
contributor runs, what each one proves, and what a result is allowed to say. A target that
returns zero without doing its work is a defect, not a pass.
.SS Source of truth
The man pages and llm.txt are the normative documentation. llm.txt is generated from the
man pages and from the upstream man pages of the packages an image actually installs, so a
behaviour is documented in one place and the two files cannot drift. readme.md is a short
entry point: what the project is, how to build it, and where the man pages are.
.PP
docs/roadmap.md is the list of what is done and what is not. Its unchecked entries are
open work, not aspiration, and an entry moves only when a test result is in hand.
.SS Building
.TP
.B make
Builds holypkg, holy-init, holyinstall and holygetiso with the ordinary CC, CPPFLAGS,
CFLAGS, LDFLAGS and LDLIBS. make CC=tcc, make CC=gcc and make CC=clang are all supported
builds, and each is expected to succeed.
.TP
.B make static STATIC_DEPS=PREFIX
Rebuilds the four binaries statically against the musl dependency prefix, which is where
the official binaries come from.
.TP
.B make bootstrap-linux VERSION=7.2.7 OUTPUT=out/linux-i686 [ARCH=i686]
Builds the kernel an image boots, from the pinned tarball in profiles/linux-sources, the
distribution defconfig that ARCH names and the recorded compiler. Every driver the profile
needs is built in, so an image carries no module tree. The tool records the config, the
tarball digest and the compiler, and refuses a kernel whose boot header names a machine
other than the one asked for.
.TP
.B make bootstrap-image ARCH=i686 KERNEL_IMAGE=... KERNEL_VERSION=... OUTPUT=...
Builds the target root, plans and applies one reviewed set, builds the initramfs, writes
the Limine configuration, produces the ISO and runs the boot test. GLIBC_CFLAGS and
MUSL_CFLAGS tell a host compiler which machine the probes it links for the image have to
produce, and each probe is read back as an ELF and refused when its machine is not the
image architecture.
.TP
.B make llm.txt
Regenerates llm.txt from the man pages. A man page edited without this target is a defect,
and tests/llm-check.sh fails the run.
.SS Tests
.TP
.B make check
The fixture suite. It needs no root and no network, and it is expected to pass.
.TP
.B make check-fixtures
The same suite, named for what it is.
.TP
.B make check-root
The root-touching fixtures, which need the caller's own privileges and write only inside a
disposable directory.
.TP
.B make check-qemu ARCH=x86_64
Boots the image that build produced, through BIOS and UEFI under TCG. ARCH=i686 boots the
32-bit target, where Limine carries BIOS only. The runner accepts a raw or qcow2 disk,
copies it to a read-only base and boots a separate overlay, gives every guest stage its own
deadline, and records the report as holy-qemu-report-2. A guest stage that never started is
reported apart from one that fell behind.
.TP
.B make check-install
The installer fixtures: plans, applies, the text menu, disk layouts, encryption and source
staging.
.TP
.B make check-hardware
The host graphics gate: a nouveau-bound NVIDIA GPU, a Mesa NVK Vulkan device, and an
accelerated OpenGL context that renders measured frames. A host without those returns
missing-requirement, never pass.
.TP
.B make check-rollback-vm DISK=PATH OTHER_KERNEL=PATH
Boots a two-slot disk under QEMU and proves the rollback contract on the machine
rather than in a config checker. Three things happen in order: the current slot stops
matching the blake2b digest its limine entry carries, so the loader warns and waits and
any key but Y returns to the menu; the replacement kernel boots, fails a guest probe and
dinit asks what to do; and after the reboot the menu comes up on the failing slot, the
highlight moves to the previous one and that slot boots with the guest probes passing,
naming the kernel release that answered. The second kernel is required input, since a
rollback between two identical kernels proves nothing. The serial line is a unix socket
because choosing a slot means pressing keys.
.TP
.B make check-matrix [ONLY=name,...] [REQUIRE=1]
Runs the compiler, SDK, language, network, system, GUI, graphics, gaming, workstation and
foreign source cases. It writes holy-matrix-report-1 to out/matrix.json: one row per case
with its argv, elapsed seconds, log path, the pinned artifact it used and the license
beside it, and one of four statuses.
.PP
A case is pass when it ran and proved its claim, fail when it ran and did not, missing when
the host has no tool it names, and unpinned when it needs an artifact that
profiles/matrix-sources carries no digest for. missing and unpinned are separate, because a
tool nobody installed and an artifact nobody wrote a digest down are different gaps. Neither
turns the run green; REQUIRE=1 makes either exit 6. A case whose remaining checks need a
tool the host lacks names that tool in its own row rather than passing quietly.
.SH WHAT A REPORT MAY CLAIM
A tool version proves the tool is present. It does not prove a library loads, a native
module resolves, a bootloader can read a filesystem, or a kernel can reach its root device.
Each of those is a separate case with its own evidence, and the report states which one ran.
.PP
Unpacking a kernel is not evidence that the kernel boots. The two are recorded as separate
results, and a boot that is not run is written as not-tested rather than inferred.
.PP
A download is not provenance. An artifact a case uses carries the digest a reviewer read at
its URL and the license beside it, and a URL that serves different bytes is a failure
rather than a newer version.
.PP
A green check suite says the fixtures passed. It says nothing about a case the suite does
not contain, which is why the roadmap lists the ones it does not.
.SH SCOPE
Read-only operations do not need root. A root phase may run hooks the user reviewed; that is
not a reason to run a compiler or a recipe evaluator as host root by default. Subprocesses
are launched through argv and never through a shell string, and sizes are checked before
an allocation or a copy.
